-- and the prediction from the configured molecule, computed: eight. Two reversals rather than valine's
-- one. The exchange of the two delta methyls reverses the arrangement at the gamma carbon, splitting the
-- six methyl hydrogens into two classes of three; the exchange of the two beta hydrogens reverses the
-- arrangement at the beta carbon, whose neighbour is the alpha stereocentre, splitting them into one
-- class each. The methyl split is in the record directly: the standard random-coil table gives leucine's
-- two delta methyls as separate resonances at 0.94 and 0.90 ppm, and the assignment of which is which
-- took the same labelling chemistry as valine's -- the method in Neri, Szyperski, Otting, Senn and
-- Wuthrich, Biochemistry 28:7510-7516 (1989), BMRB entry 195, and the shifts for valyl and leucyl
-- residues compiled in Neri, Otting and Wuthrich, Tetrahedron 46:3287-3296 (1990), BMRB entry 486. The
-- beta split is predicted here and not visible in that table, which lists one value for the beta
-- methylene, 1.65 ppm; in proteins the two beta protons resolve and are stereospecifically assigned
-- routinely, and calculation against crystal coordinates recovers the assignment about four times in
-- five (Williamson, FEBS Letters 302:185-188, 1992). Eight is the count of classes, and a spectrum
-- showing seven peaks is a spectrum in which two classes coincide -- which is the next distinction the
-- construction has to carry, below.
lu-signals-conf : class-count Leu lu-eq lu-rank lu-conf-gens 22 lu-h ≡ 8
lu-signals-conf = λ i → 8
